1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,010
Imagine at 9 o'clock tomorrow morning, the
moon stops holding its orbit.

2
00:00:06,010 --> 00:00:13,170
There is no explosion, no collision, no warning on the news, because for the first
few days

3
00:00:13,170 --> 00:00:15,090
there is nothing to report.

4
00:00:15,090 --> 00:00:20,590
The moon simply stops moving sideways fast enough to stay where it is, and begins
very

5
00:00:20,590 --> 00:00:23,050
slowly to fall.

6
00:00:23,050 --> 00:00:27,990
You will not die in the impact, almost
nobody will.

7
00:00:27,990 --> 00:00:30,940
That is not how this ends.

8
00:00:30,940 --> 00:00:37,780
What actually happens is stranger than a crash, and the last part of it lasts a
million years.

9
00:00:37,780 --> 00:00:41,000
Let's be honest about one thing first.

10
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:43,680
There is no mechanism for this.

11
00:00:43,680 --> 00:00:46,020
The moon is not falling.

12
00:00:46,020 --> 00:00:52,380
It is doing the opposite, drifting away from us, at about 3.8 cm a year, roughly
the

13
00:00:52,380 --> 00:00:54,760
rate your fingernails grow.

14
00:00:54,760 --> 00:00:59,820
To make it fall, you would have to take away most of its orbital speed, and
nothing in the

15
00:00:59,820 --> 00:01:03,040
solar system can do that.

16
00:01:03,040 --> 00:01:08,320
So treat the premise as a thought experiment with the physics left switched
on.

17
00:01:08,320 --> 00:01:10,980
The moon starts coming down.

18
00:01:10,980 --> 00:01:16,360
Everything after that is just gravity doing exactly what gravity does, and the
numbers

19
00:01:16,360 --> 00:01:18,800
are not hard to follow.

20
00:01:18,800 --> 00:01:24,000
The moon sits about 384,000 km away.

21
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:27,840
It weighs about 73 billion billion tons.

22
00:01:27,840 --> 00:01:31,640
And the single most important thing to
understand is this.

23
00:01:31,640 --> 00:01:36,560
The force it exerts on you does not fade
in a straight line as it approaches.

24
00:01:36,560 --> 00:01:40,440
Tidal force scales with the inverse cube
of distance.

25
00:01:40,440 --> 00:01:46,520
Cut the distance in half, and the pool that stretches our planet becomes 8 times
stronger.

26
00:01:46,520 --> 00:01:51,310
Cut it to a tenth, and it is a thousand
times stronger.

27
00:01:51,310 --> 00:01:53,030
Look onto that number.

28
00:01:53,030 --> 00:01:56,010
It is the whole story.

29
00:01:56,010 --> 00:01:58,120
Nothing happens.

30
00:01:58,120 --> 00:02:01,240
This is the part that would really get to
people.

31
00:02:01,240 --> 00:02:06,640
For the first several days, the moon looks slightly larger each night, and that is
all.

32
00:02:06,640 --> 00:02:10,860
The change is so gradual that you would
argue about it.

33
00:02:10,860 --> 00:02:17,080
Someone photographs it against the same chimney two nights running and posts the
comparison.

34
00:02:17,080 --> 00:02:21,100
Half the replies say it is a lens
artifact.

35
00:02:21,140 --> 00:02:24,020
Astronomers know within hours.

36
00:02:24,020 --> 00:02:30,340
The orbital tracking data is unambiguous, and by the second day every observatory on
Earth

37
00:02:30,340 --> 00:02:34,620
is pointed at the same object, publishing
the same curve.

38
00:02:34,620 --> 00:02:39,020
But the public conversation stays stuck on
whether the moon looks bigger, because at

39
00:02:39,020 --> 00:02:43,620
this distance the honest answer is,
barely.

40
00:02:43,620 --> 00:02:47,240
Meanwhile the ocean already knows.

41
00:02:47,240 --> 00:02:49,920
The tides are a tug of war.

42
00:02:49,920 --> 00:02:55,520
The moon pulls hardest on the water nearest it and least on the water on the
far side, and

43
00:02:55,520 --> 00:03:01,880
the planet gets stretched into a slight rugby ball shape with a bulge of ocean on
each side.

44
00:03:01,880 --> 00:03:05,200
That is why you get two high tides a day,
not one.

45
00:03:05,200 --> 00:03:11,200
As the moon closes in, that stretch gets
stronger in a hurry because of the cube.

46
00:03:11,200 --> 00:03:16,760
By the end of week one, the moon is close enough that spring tides, the big ones,
start

47
00:03:16,800 --> 00:03:20,360
running measurably higher than the tide
tables say.

48
00:03:20,360 --> 00:03:27,520
Harbour masters notice before anyone else,
not a disaster, a discrepancy.

49
00:03:27,520 --> 00:03:32,760
Water arriving where the charts say it should not be twice a day every day, and
the gap

50
00:03:32,760 --> 00:03:35,390
widening each time.

51
00:03:35,390 --> 00:03:39,410
And this is where the story stops being
quiet.

52
00:03:39,410 --> 00:03:42,810
The moon is now half its original
distance.

53
00:03:42,810 --> 00:03:48,210
It is twice the width it used to be in the
sky, and it is bright enough to read by.

54
00:03:48,210 --> 00:03:52,560
People stop arguing about whether it looks
bigger.

55
00:03:52,560 --> 00:03:55,520
Tides are eight times stronger.

56
00:03:55,520 --> 00:03:59,960
That sounds survivable until you think
about what a tide actually is.

57
00:03:59,960 --> 00:04:04,320
A high tide of two metres becomes a high
tide of sixteen.

58
00:04:04,320 --> 00:04:08,680
The sea does not rise and stay risen,
which is somehow worse.

59
00:04:08,680 --> 00:04:13,360
It comes in, floods everything below that
line, and goes back out.

60
00:04:13,360 --> 00:04:17,320
And it does it again about twelve and a
half hours later.

61
00:04:17,320 --> 00:04:20,960
Coastal cities are not destroyed on day
nineteen.

62
00:04:20,960 --> 00:04:26,260
They are soaked, drained, and soaked
again, and nothing ever dries.

63
00:04:26,260 --> 00:04:29,980
Every low-lying river mouth in the world
backs up.

64
00:04:29,980 --> 00:04:32,540
Storm drains run in reverse.

65
00:04:32,540 --> 00:04:38,820
The water coming into cities is salt water, so the first serious casualty is
not buildings.

66
00:04:38,820 --> 00:04:41,900
It is farmland and fresh water.

67
00:04:41,900 --> 00:04:48,660
Rivers near any coast begin taking on salt, and once that happens, you cannot
undo it.

68
00:04:48,660 --> 00:04:54,020
Seventy percent of this planet's surface is ocean, and about forty percent of
humanity

69
00:04:54,020 --> 00:04:57,860
lives within a hundred kilometres of a
coastline.

70
00:04:57,860 --> 00:05:02,940
That is roughly three billion people, and
a very large share of the world's ports,

71
00:05:02,940 --> 00:05:08,020
refineries, power stations, and chip fabs,
all of which were built at sea level on

72
00:05:08,020 --> 00:05:09,830
purpose.

73
00:05:09,830 --> 00:05:16,610
And somewhere above all this, we lose the machinery we would need to coordinate a
response.

74
00:05:16,610 --> 00:05:21,450
The moon, on its way down, sweeps through
the region where our satellites live.

75
00:05:21,450 --> 00:05:28,050
Geostationary satellites, the ones that carry a great deal of broadcasting and
weather observation,

76
00:05:28,050 --> 00:05:31,650
sit about thirty-five thousand eight
hundred kilometres up.

77
00:05:31,650 --> 00:05:35,010
The moon passes straight through that
altitude.

78
00:05:35,010 --> 00:05:37,130
It does not need to hit anything.

79
00:05:37,130 --> 00:05:42,970
A mass that large moving through that volume wrecks the orbits of everything
near it, and

80
00:05:42,970 --> 00:05:47,700
the debris from the collisions it does
cause finishes the rest.

81
00:05:47,700 --> 00:05:53,260
Lower down, the navigation constellations at around twenty thousand kilometres go
the

82
00:05:53,260 --> 00:05:55,220
same way.

83
00:05:55,220 --> 00:06:00,860
So in the middle of the largest coordinated evacuation in history,
precision timing

84
00:06:00,860 --> 00:06:03,620
and navigation stop working.

85
00:06:03,620 --> 00:06:10,140
Not just maps on phones, those satellite clocks underpin power grid
synchronisation, financial

86
00:06:10,140 --> 00:06:16,020
transaction time stamps, aviation,
shipping, and the mobile network itself.

87
00:06:16,020 --> 00:06:21,980
The infrastructure we would use to organise and orderly retreat inland is
tied to hardware

88
00:06:21,980 --> 00:06:26,620
that is now being scattered by the thing
we are retreating from.

89
00:06:26,620 --> 00:06:29,780
But the ocean is not the dangerous part.

90
00:06:29,780 --> 00:06:35,220
The dangerous part is quieter and it is
happening underneath.

91
00:06:35,220 --> 00:06:40,820
Tidal force does not only move water, it
moves rock.

92
00:06:40,820 --> 00:06:46,460
Right now, today, the solid ground under your feet rises and falls by roughly
thirty to

93
00:06:46,460 --> 00:06:48,980
forty centimetres twice a day.

94
00:06:48,980 --> 00:06:53,140
You cannot feel it because everything
around you moves with it.

95
00:06:53,140 --> 00:06:58,540
This is called the earth tide and it is
real, measurable and completely ignorable

96
00:06:58,580 --> 00:07:01,070
under normal conditions.

97
00:07:01,070 --> 00:07:05,710
Multiply it by eight, then keep going.

98
00:07:05,710 --> 00:07:09,070
Rock is not elastic in a friendly way.

99
00:07:09,070 --> 00:07:11,910
Flex it repeatedly and it does not bend.

100
00:07:11,910 --> 00:07:15,150
It accumulates stress until something
slips.

101
00:07:15,150 --> 00:07:20,590
Every fault line on earth is now being worked twice a day by a force that is
growing

102
00:07:20,590 --> 00:07:21,910
week by week.

103
00:07:21,910 --> 00:07:26,190
Faults that were due to go in ten thousand
years go this month.

104
00:07:26,230 --> 00:07:29,400
Faults that were locked go anyway.

105
00:07:29,400 --> 00:07:35,160
This is the first stage that kills large numbers of people and it does it inland,
far from

106
00:07:35,160 --> 00:07:41,060
the water, in places that thought they were safe because they were nowhere near a
coast.

107
00:07:41,060 --> 00:07:48,700
The flexing also reaches the magma, volcanoes that have been dormant for
centuries start venting.

108
00:07:48,700 --> 00:07:53,380
We know this mechanism is real because we
can watch it happen elsewhere.

109
00:07:53,380 --> 00:07:59,060
Jupiter's moon Io is squeezed by exactly this kind of tidal kneading and it is the
most

110
00:07:59,060 --> 00:08:05,260
volcanically active object in the solar system with hundreds of active volcanoes
and plumes

111
00:08:05,260 --> 00:08:09,020
that throw material hundreds of kilometers
into space.

112
00:08:09,020 --> 00:08:11,220
Io is not a theory.

113
00:08:11,220 --> 00:08:17,740
It is a worked example of what happens when something gets flexed hard enough,
long enough.

114
00:08:17,740 --> 00:08:20,180
Earth will not become Io.

115
00:08:20,180 --> 00:08:22,900
It will not get the chance.

116
00:08:22,940 --> 00:08:28,940
Now the moon is genuinely close and the sky is wrong in a way that no photograph
has ever

117
00:08:28,940 --> 00:08:30,260
shown.

118
00:08:30,260 --> 00:08:32,860
Io is no longer a disk.

119
00:08:32,860 --> 00:08:35,220
Io is a landscape overhead.

120
00:08:35,220 --> 00:08:39,900
You can see craters with your eyes that
previously needed a telescope.

121
00:08:39,900 --> 00:08:42,660
It covers a visible fraction of the sky.

122
00:08:42,660 --> 00:08:48,300
It moves perceptibly against the stars while you watch and at night it is bright
enough

123
00:08:48,300 --> 00:08:54,430
that there is no real darkness anywhere on
the half of the planet facing it.

124
00:08:54,430 --> 00:08:57,870
Tides are now hundreds of times their
normal strength.

125
00:08:57,870 --> 00:09:01,510
There is no coastline left in any
meaningful sense.

126
00:09:01,510 --> 00:09:07,510
The sea is sloshing across continental shelves twice a day in a way that has no
modern

127
00:09:07,510 --> 00:09:12,510
precedent, because the last time anything
like this happened the continents were in

128
00:09:12,510 --> 00:09:14,760
different places.

129
00:09:14,760 --> 00:09:17,800
The atmosphere is being pulled too.

130
00:09:17,800 --> 00:09:24,240
There is a fluid and it has tides exactly like the ocean does normally too small to
notice.

131
00:09:24,240 --> 00:09:30,360
Now there are atmospheric tides strong enough to drive continent scale whether on
a 12-hour

132
00:09:30,360 --> 00:09:31,710
cycle.

133
00:09:31,710 --> 00:09:35,600
And everyone is waiting for the impact.

134
00:09:35,600 --> 00:09:38,700
The impact never comes.

135
00:09:38,700 --> 00:09:41,220
The moon does not hit the earth.

136
00:09:41,220 --> 00:09:42,660
It cannot.

137
00:09:42,660 --> 00:09:47,940
It is destroyed before it gets the chance
by the same force that has been causing

138
00:09:47,940 --> 00:09:49,980
all the trouble.

139
00:09:49,980 --> 00:09:52,820
There is a distance called the Roche
limit.

140
00:09:52,820 --> 00:09:57,860
It is the point where the tidal stretching
across an object becomes stronger than the

141
00:09:57,860 --> 00:10:01,260
object's own gravity holding it together.

142
00:10:01,260 --> 00:10:06,380
Inside that distance a body held together mainly by its own weight cannot stay in
one

143
00:10:06,380 --> 00:10:07,380
piece.

144
00:10:07,380 --> 00:10:13,060
The near side is being pulled toward the planet harder than the far side and
eventually

145
00:10:13,060 --> 00:10:15,350
the difference wins.

146
00:10:15,350 --> 00:10:20,990
For the moon approaching earth that boundary sits somewhere between about 9
and a half thousand

147
00:10:20,990 --> 00:10:24,510
and 18 thousand kilometers from earth's
center.

148
00:10:24,510 --> 00:10:28,990
Which end of that range applies depends on
how much credit you give the moon's own

149
00:10:28,990 --> 00:10:30,470
rock strength.

150
00:10:30,470 --> 00:10:36,150
A rigid body holds together closer in, a
body already cracking apart comes undone

151
00:10:36,150 --> 00:10:37,790
further out.

152
00:10:37,790 --> 00:10:42,670
Measured from the ground, even the near
end is thousands of kilometers up.

153
00:10:42,670 --> 00:10:47,310
Further than the navigation satellites it
has already wrecked on the way down, far

154
00:10:47,310 --> 00:10:54,000
higher than any mountain and crucially a
long way from ever touching the surface.

155
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:58,420
So the moon arrives at that line and it
comes apart.

156
00:10:58,420 --> 00:11:01,100
Not instantly and not cleanly.

157
00:11:01,100 --> 00:11:06,100
It elongates first stretching toward us
its near side bulging.

158
00:11:06,100 --> 00:11:08,340
Then the crust fails.

159
00:11:08,340 --> 00:11:14,140
Earth's fractures open across a world whose great volcanic era ended two to
three billion

160
00:11:14,140 --> 00:11:15,300
years ago.

161
00:11:15,300 --> 00:11:21,020
The whole body pulls into a stream of rock still moving at orbital speed still
traveling

162
00:11:21,020 --> 00:11:24,740
the same path but no longer a moon.

163
00:11:24,740 --> 00:11:28,370
In a matter of days earth has a ring.

164
00:11:28,370 --> 00:11:35,090
Let's slow down because the breakup itself
is the part worth watching frame by frame.

165
00:11:35,090 --> 00:11:39,530
In the first seconds from the ground
almost nothing looks different.

166
00:11:39,530 --> 00:11:44,050
Light takes a few hundredths of a second to reach you from that distance so you see
it

167
00:11:44,050 --> 00:11:49,890
essentially as it happens but what you see
at first is only the shape going wrong.

168
00:11:49,890 --> 00:11:52,230
The disk stops being round.

169
00:11:52,230 --> 00:11:55,290
One edge pulls toward you.

170
00:11:55,290 --> 00:11:59,690
Within the first minutes the fractures are
visible as dark lines.

171
00:11:59,690 --> 00:12:04,690
There is no sound and there will never be
any sound because there is no air between

172
00:12:04,690 --> 00:12:06,050
you and it.

173
00:12:06,050 --> 00:12:11,850
This is a catastrophe happening in complete silence at a scale that fills the
sky and

174
00:12:11,850 --> 00:12:16,180
that silence is the detail people would
remember.

175
00:12:16,180 --> 00:12:19,300
Within the first hours the stream is
obvious.

176
00:12:19,300 --> 00:12:24,380
What was a single object is now a thickening band brighter along its length
as fresh

177
00:12:24,380 --> 00:12:27,100
surfaces catch the sunlight.

178
00:12:27,100 --> 00:12:31,420
Lunar rock that has not seen the sun in
four and a half billion years is now

179
00:12:31,460 --> 00:12:38,310
exposed and the whole structure is noticeably brighter than the moon ever
was.

180
00:12:38,310 --> 00:12:42,470
Within the first day the leading edge of the debris has stretched far enough around
the

181
00:12:42,470 --> 00:12:47,910
planet that it rises in the east while the
trailing material is still setting in the

182
00:12:47,910 --> 00:12:48,910
west.

183
00:12:48,910 --> 00:12:53,550
The sky now has a structure in it that was
not there yesterday.

184
00:12:53,550 --> 00:12:59,650
And the ground is still moving breaking
the moon apart did not remove its mass.

185
00:12:59,650 --> 00:13:04,850
The pieces are still there, still pulling, still stretching the planet twice a day
and

186
00:13:04,850 --> 00:13:11,130
they are now spread across a range of distances so the tidal forcing becomes
messy and irregular

187
00:13:11,130 --> 00:13:14,590
rather than a clean twice daily rhythm.

188
00:13:14,590 --> 00:13:17,610
The debris does not fall straight down.

189
00:13:17,610 --> 00:13:22,570
It keeps orbiting because it inherited the
moon's motion and it spreads out along

190
00:13:22,570 --> 00:13:27,630
that orbit until it forms a band
encircling the planet.

191
00:13:27,630 --> 00:13:31,230
If you have seen Saturn you already know
the shape.

192
00:13:31,230 --> 00:13:38,510
Saturn's rings are mostly water ice, they are over 280,000 km across and in places
they

193
00:13:38,510 --> 00:13:40,950
are only about 10 meters thick.

194
00:13:40,950 --> 00:13:45,990
They are a demonstration that this is the natural end state for a lot of debris
sharing

195
00:13:45,990 --> 00:13:50,760
one orbit, not a cloud, not a shell, but a
disk.

196
00:13:50,760 --> 00:13:56,280
Earth's ring would be rock rather than
ice, so darker but it would be close which

197
00:13:56,280 --> 00:13:58,640
makes it enormous in the sky.

198
00:13:58,640 --> 00:14:03,240
It would arch from horizon to horizon as a
band of light.

199
00:14:03,240 --> 00:14:08,400
Everyone on the planet would be able to see it day and night, although from high
latitudes

200
00:14:08,400 --> 00:14:13,320
you would see it at a low angle and from
the equator it would pass almost directly

201
00:14:13,320 --> 00:14:15,150
overhead.

202
00:14:15,150 --> 00:14:17,430
It would also cast a shadow.

203
00:14:17,430 --> 00:14:23,470
A band of shade would sweep across the planet's surface seasonally, cooling
whatever it crossed

204
00:14:23,470 --> 00:14:25,470
and it would do something uglier.

205
00:14:25,510 --> 00:14:30,510
It would scatter sunlight into the night
side so true darkness would become rare.

206
00:14:30,510 --> 00:14:36,350
Every nocturnal ecosystem on Earth, which is most of them, runs on a cycle that no
longer

207
00:14:36,350 --> 00:14:38,140
exists.

208
00:14:38,140 --> 00:14:44,200
A ring is not permanent, the inner edge is
the problem.

209
00:14:44,200 --> 00:14:49,280
Material at the inner edge is slowly dragged down by the last traces of
atmosphere and

210
00:14:49,280 --> 00:14:52,680
by collisions with its neighbors and it
falls.

211
00:14:52,680 --> 00:14:58,100
Not all at once, continuously for a very
long time.

212
00:14:58,100 --> 00:15:01,700
So Earth gets a bombardment that does not
stop.

213
00:15:01,700 --> 00:15:06,560
Not the single catastrophic impact everyone imagines when they think about
things falling

214
00:15:06,560 --> 00:15:13,060
from space, but a steady reign of lunar rock, ranging from dust to pieces the size
of

215
00:15:13,060 --> 00:15:18,600
buildings, arriving somewhere on the
planet every day for thousands of years.

216
00:15:18,600 --> 00:15:21,920
The dust matters more than the big pieces.

217
00:15:21,920 --> 00:15:27,960
Even material entering the atmosphere at orbital speed does not reach the ground as
rock.

218
00:15:27,960 --> 00:15:33,240
It burns and that puts a persistent haze high in the atmosphere where weather
cannot wash

219
00:15:33,240 --> 00:15:34,640
it out.

220
00:15:34,640 --> 00:15:40,000
Sunlight drops, temperatures drop,
photosynthesis drops.

221
00:15:40,000 --> 00:15:45,080
The planet cools and stays cool and the
surface becomes a place where the sky is

222
00:15:45,080 --> 00:15:51,260
permanently bright with a ring and permanently dimmed by what the ring is
shedding.

223
00:15:51,260 --> 00:15:55,420
This is the stage that actually decides
what survives.

224
00:15:55,420 --> 00:15:58,740
Not the tides, not the earthquakes, not
the breakup.

225
00:15:58,740 --> 00:16:02,430
The long grey centuries afterwards.

226
00:16:02,430 --> 00:16:08,030
And it is worth being clear about who survives it because the answer is not the
one disaster

227
00:16:08,030 --> 00:16:10,200
films give you.

228
00:16:10,200 --> 00:16:15,480
The ocean takes the coasts so the
survivors are inland and at altitude.

229
00:16:15,480 --> 00:16:20,200
The earthquakes take the cities so the
survivors are in low buildings.

230
00:16:20,200 --> 00:16:25,480
The haze takes the harvests so the survivors are wherever food can still be
grown under

231
00:16:25,480 --> 00:16:29,920
a dimmer sun or stored long enough to
outlast the worst of it.

232
00:16:29,920 --> 00:16:35,160
None of that favors the places where most of us currently live because most of us
live

233
00:16:35,160 --> 00:16:42,370
near water in dense construction eating
food grown somewhere else and shipped in.

234
00:16:42,370 --> 00:16:45,890
Life itself is never seriously at risk
here.

235
00:16:45,890 --> 00:16:52,010
Most ocean ecosystems around hydrothermal vents do not care about sunlight at all
and they

236
00:16:52,010 --> 00:16:54,690
would carry on exactly as before.

237
00:16:54,690 --> 00:16:58,850
Plenty of microbial life would not
register the event.

238
00:16:58,850 --> 00:17:05,250
What is at risk is the specific fragile highly coordinated arrangement that lets 8
billion

239
00:17:05,250 --> 00:17:07,940
people eat every day.

240
00:17:07,940 --> 00:17:11,060
Eventually, the ring thins out.

241
00:17:11,060 --> 00:17:16,460
Most of it has either fallen or been flung outward and what is left is a faint band
far

242
00:17:16,460 --> 00:17:19,190
less dramatic than it was.

243
00:17:19,190 --> 00:17:23,190
The days are shorter now and they stay
shorter.

244
00:17:23,190 --> 00:17:28,190
The moon has been slowing earth's rotation
for billions of years.

245
00:17:28,190 --> 00:17:33,310
Tidal friction drags on our spin and hands
that energy to the moon's orbit which

246
00:17:33,310 --> 00:17:38,390
is exactly why the moon is drifting away
and why our day has been lengthening by

247
00:17:38,390 --> 00:17:41,390
roughly 2 milliseconds per century.

248
00:17:41,390 --> 00:17:46,870
Tidal coral growth bands suggest that a day several hundred million years ago was
around

249
00:17:46,870 --> 00:17:49,710
22 hours long.

250
00:17:49,710 --> 00:17:52,550
Remove the brake and the lengthening
stops.

251
00:17:52,550 --> 00:17:57,630
The day freezes at whatever length it had
reached and then the ring material falling

252
00:17:57,630 --> 00:18:00,230
back in nudges it the other way.

253
00:18:00,230 --> 00:18:05,470
A planet that had been slowly winding down
stops winding down.

254
00:18:05,470 --> 00:18:10,830
And earth without a large moon is a different planet in one specific way that
matters

255
00:18:10,830 --> 00:18:13,250
more than it sounds.

256
00:18:13,250 --> 00:18:17,090
Our moon helps hold the tilt of earth's
axis steady.

257
00:18:17,090 --> 00:18:22,460
That tilt about 23.5 degrees is what gives
us seasons.

258
00:18:22,460 --> 00:18:27,980
Now how much the moon actually matters here is a genuine argument among people
who model

259
00:18:27,980 --> 00:18:32,140
this for a living and I am not going to
pretend it is settled.

260
00:18:32,140 --> 00:18:37,580
The classic result from the early 90s found that without the moon our tilt could
swing

261
00:18:37,580 --> 00:18:40,340
chaotically through tens of degrees.

262
00:18:40,340 --> 00:18:46,700
A later generation of simulations came back with something much milder, a
moonless earth

263
00:18:46,700 --> 00:18:52,740
wandering within a range of perhaps 20 or
25 degrees rather than running wild.

264
00:18:52,740 --> 00:18:58,820
Mars with only two tiny captured moons really does swing widely which is part of
why the

265
00:18:58,820 --> 00:19:02,540
original result looked so convincing.

266
00:19:02,540 --> 00:19:09,420
So the honest version is this, losing the moon does not instantly scramble the
seasons.

267
00:19:09,420 --> 00:19:11,240
It removes a break.

268
00:19:11,240 --> 00:19:16,780
The tilt becomes freer to wander over millions of years and climate zones that
have been roughly

269
00:19:16,780 --> 00:19:21,580
where they are for the whole of human
history stop being guaranteed.

270
00:19:21,580 --> 00:19:27,260
Whether that is a slow drift or a wild ride is exactly the part the models still
disagree

271
00:19:27,260 --> 00:19:28,820
about.

272
00:19:28,820 --> 00:19:32,340
So the moon falling does not end with a
crater.

273
00:19:32,340 --> 00:19:38,020
It ends with a planet that has a ring, a
ruined coastline, a cooler sky and no

274
00:19:38,020 --> 00:19:41,380
longer keeps time the way it used to.

275
00:19:41,380 --> 00:19:44,740
The strangest part of all this is the
first week.

276
00:19:44,740 --> 00:19:50,180
The part where the data is already in, every scientist on earth agrees and the
view out

277
00:19:50,180 --> 00:19:53,340
of the window still looks completely
normal.

278
00:19:53,340 --> 00:19:59,910
That gap between knowing and seeing would
be the most human part of the whole event.

279
00:19:59,910 --> 00:20:03,030
Here is the question I want to leave you
with.

280
00:20:03,030 --> 00:20:07,870
Given that the first week looks like nothing at all and the last week is
unsurvivable

281
00:20:07,870 --> 00:20:13,470
near any coast, when exactly do you think
people would actually start moving inland?

282
00:20:13,470 --> 00:20:20,460
On the day the astronomers announce it, or on the day they can finally see it for
themselves.

283
00:20:20,460 --> 00:20:21,900
Tell me in the comments.
